{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high in the first ~1–10 days (+~0.3 to +1.5 €/t typical), then become materially too low in strong uptrends after ~day 20 (miss late-stage rally by ~+1 to +5 €/t). Apply a bias curve that starts mildly negative (reduce forecast) and turns positive beyond ~day 20 in rising regimes.",
    "Turning-point/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts—after a dip it keeps prices too high for ~1–2 weeks; after an upswing starts it underestimates the persistence/acceleration beyond ~2–4 weeks. Add a momentum/regime filter: if last 10–15d slope > 0, boost outer-horizon; if slope < 0, cut near-horizon more aggressively.",
    "Overshoot/undershoot around spikes: when the model projects sharp late-horizon spikes (e.g., >~+5 €/t above recent mean), it tends to overshoot the peak level by ~0.5–2 €/t and/or peak too early. Cap projected deviations from a rolling mean/median and smooth the last-third of the path (reduce curvature).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: mid-horizon (≈days 10–20) reversion is too strong—forecasts drift back toward mid-60s even when realized prices stay elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when spot is above its 20–30d average and the average is rising.",
    "Volatility term-structure: forecast volatility is too high late-horizon (excessive swings), while realized paths are smoother. Apply variance shrinkage with horizon (progressively damp daily step changes after ~day 10)."
  ]
}
